
  [1mThe fork:[0m what happens to a reminder that comes due when the app isn't open?

  - [1mFires only while open.[0m A timer in the page checks due times and raises a notification. Zero infrastructure, works in
    every browser. The honest limitation: close the tab and nothing fires — you'd never hear about tomorrow's task
    unless you happened to be looking at the app.
  - [1mCatch-up on open.[0m No background work at all. When you open the app it surfaces everything that came due while you
    were away. Fully deterministic, works everywhere, never misses anything — but it's a digest you pull, not an alert
    that reaches you.
  - [1mService worker background.[0m Installable PWA using Periodic Background Sync to fire with the tab closed. This is the
    only option that's a real push, and it's the one I'd warn you about: it's Chromium-only, absent on iOS Safari and
    Firefox, requires the user to install the app, and the browser decides whether and when to run it based on
    engagement heuristics. You'd be building a notification system that silently doesn't fire for a large share of users
    and can't be debugged from the outside.

  My recommendation is [1mthe first two together[0m. They're complementary rather than competing — live firing handles the
  case where you're using the app, catch-up guarantees nothing is ever silently dropped, and together they're a coherent
  story with no "sometimes it works" caveat. Both are cheap and neither constrains the other. If you later want a
  genuine buzz-at-9am notification, the correct answer is a server, and that's a separate project with its own design —
  not something to approximate badly here.
